08b3fd06aec2b9c2c141da2409514d8598e99d9ded" "basis" 335 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931816/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-250517" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If the expression ${x}^{2}-10x$ is written as $(x-p{)}^{2}+q$, then what are the values of $p$ and $q?$\n\n$p=$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,q=$\n{{field-2}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931816/state.json" "file_sha256" "927e24c36de12d5d49faeb08b3fd06aec2b9c2c141da2409514d8598e99d9ded" "basis" 335 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931816/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-250517" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 5x+0\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${5}^{2},$ writing the constant term $0$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 5x+{5}^{2}}_{⏟}-\\,{5}^{2}+0\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-5{)}^{2}-{5}^{2}+0\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x-5{)}^{2}-25\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=5$ and $q=-25.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931816/state.json" "file_sha256" "927e24c36de12d5d49faeb08b3fd06aec2b9c2c141da2409514d8598e99d9ded" "basis" 335 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931816/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-250517" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931816/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3e2580e130e3316029db96b76a6dfc8a419f7b6adb